title: Keybinding variables
tags: Keybindings Configuration
kind: reference
summary: A letter named once in keybindings.toml: variables in the keys, and {target} templates that write the here / beside / new variants.

The shipped keys are built from a few letters with a role — a domain such as `,`
for the tags, a verb such as `a` for ''add'', a target prefix such as `i` for
''here''. `gui/keybindings.toml` names each of those letters once, so that moving
a role to another letter is one line, not fifty.

!! Variables

The table `vars` maps a name to keys, and `{name}` in a key of the file stands for
them:

```
vars.tags = ","
vars.add = "a"
vars.bulk-query = "m q"

"{tags} {add}" = { command = "mf:tag add" }
"{bulk-query} d" = { command = "metarecord:bulk query delete" }
"shift+{add}" = { command = "…" }
```

* A variable holds a key or a sequence (`"m q"`); its name is lowercase letters, digits and `-`.
* A value may name other variables: `vars.negative = "{tags} -"` holds `, -` while `tags` is `,`, and follows it when it moves — which is why the shipped `checked`, `query`, `items`, `negative`… are written on `record`, `item` and `tags`. Variables that refer to themselves, through any number of others, are an error that names them.
* The table can be written as `vars.name = …` lines anywhere at the top of the file, or as a `[vars]` section at its end — TOML puts every key after a section header into that section.
* A `{name}` that is not a variable is an error, which names it. A brace that names nothing (`"{"`) is a key like any other.
* Variables are replaced in the keys only, not in the commands.

!! Target templates

Many commands take a target — where a panel opens: `here`, `other` (the other
slot) or `new` (a new workspace). A binding whose command holds `{target}` and
which names its default `target` stands for four:

```
vars.target-here = "i"
vars.target-other = "b"
vars.target-new = "n"

"t l" = { command = "panel:open {target} metarecord-list", target = "other" }
```

binds `t l` to `panel:open other metarecord-list`, and `i t l`, `b t l`, `n t l`
to the same command aimed `here`, `other` and `new`. The three prefixes are the
variables `target-here`, `target-other` and `target-new`; a template without
them, without a default `target`, or a `target` with no `{target}` to fill is an
error.

!! Changing one of them

The [[keybindings-variables panel]] lists the variables, each with its value, the keys it stands
for and how many lines use it; a new value typed there is written into its line of
the file, and every key built on it moves. It is refused when it would bind a key
twice in one place, or make a key alone hide a sequence that nothing hid before — `go`
given the value `x` would hide every `x …` behind the command input's `x`.

The panel lists the ''lines'' of the file: a line using a variable or a template is one
row, its key as written beside the keys it binds. Editing the row edits the line, so a
template's four keys move together, and removing it removes them all.

A key a template produced can still be given a meaning of its own: a ''literal'' key of
the file, in the same place, overrides it, and the template's other keys stay as they
are. Two lines producing the same key in the same place — two templates, or two
literal keys — are refused, as always.

[[Keyboard navigation]] gives the keys as they are shipped, variables expanded,
and the generated notes of each command list its keys the same way.
